---
// /about/ (rebuild plan §4.2). Where the office says the most about itself
// (docs/VOICE.md, "Describe the office when it helps"), kept to facts with a
// source each. Staff, partners and projects have their own pages, from the
// content collections.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/paths';
---
<Base short="About"
      description="The Montana Climate Office at the University of Montana: what we do, and how to reach us.">
  <!-- Copy drafted with AI assistance; needs MCO staff review before launch (UM AI disclosure). -->
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About the Montana Climate Office</h1>

    {/* Designation: mco-website/_pages/about/about.md ("In 2006 Governor
        Schweitzer designated the Montana Climate Office, at the University
        of Montana, as the official state climate office"; "an independent
        state-designated body"). College and town: the site footer, owner
        decision of 22 Oct 2026 (rebuild plan Phase 1 item 3; DECISIONS D15
        postal address). */}
    <p>
      The Montana Climate Office is Montana's state climate office. Governor Schweitzer designated
      it in 2006, at the University of Montana, as an independent office serving the whole state.
      We work out of the W.A. Franke College of Forestry &amp; Conservation in Missoula.
    </p>

    {/* Two basins and work past the state line: mco-website/_pages/about/
        about.md ("at the nexus of two of America's great river basins, the
        Columbia and the Missouri"; "many of the Montana Climate Office
        products extend to other regions"). */}
    <p>
      Montana holds the headwaters of two of the country's largest river basins, the Columbia and
      the Missouri. Water that falls here ends up a long way downstream, so a good deal of our work
      reaches past the state line.
    </p>

    <h2>What we do</h2>
    {/* Operates the Mesonet: mco-website/index.md ("stations that the MCO
        operates across Montana"). First stations 2016 and the Corps of
        Engineers build: src/pages/mesonet/index.astro. Visits: the Station
        Maintenance tool (src/lib/tools.ts). Quality control:
        src/pages/mesonet/qaqc.astro. */}
    <p>
      We run the Montana Mesonet, the statewide network of weather and soil moisture stations we
      have been building since 2016. Our field staff install
      the stations and go back to maintain them, and every reading goes through quality-control checks
      before it settles into the record.
    </p>

    {/* The tools: src/lib/tools.ts, repos under mt-climate-office
        (phase-0-report §3). */}
    <p>
      We also build the web tools that put that data on a map, along with national drought and
      snowpack data. The Mesonet Explorer, the Station Dashboard, the Snowpack Explorer and the
      D³ Drought Data Dashboard are all ours. The <a href={url('conditions/')}>Conditions</a>,{' '}
      <a href={url('drought/')}>Drought</a> and <a href={url('mesonet/')}>Mesonet</a> pages say what
      each one is for.
    </p>

    {/* Interpreting climate information: mco-website/_pages/about/about.md
        ("assist stakeholders in interpreting climate information or adapting
        climate products to their needs"). */}
    <p>
      Part of a state climate office's job is helping people read climate information and fit it to
      their own work. Anyone with a question about a map or a number on this site can write to us,
      and we will explain it.
    </p>

    {/* MSDI stewardship: src/content/projects/msl.md. AASC membership and
        federal partners: src/content/partners/{aasc,noaa}.md. */}
    <p>
      Since 2013 we have been the state's official steward for climate data in the Montana Spatial
      Data Infrastructure. We are a member of the American Association of State Climatologists and,
      like the other state climate offices, work with NOAA, its Regional Climate Centers and the
      National Weather Service.
    </p>

    <h2>People, partners and projects</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={url('about/team/')}>team page</a> introduces the staff, students and affiliates
      who work here. Our <a href={url('about/partnerships/')}>partners and sponsors</a> are the
      organizations we work with and the agencies that fund the work. The research we do with other
      universities and agencies is on the <a href={url('projects/')}>projects</a> page.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    {/* Address and email: the site footer (rebuild plan Phase 1 item 3). */}
    <p>
      Email reaches the whole office at{' '}
      <a href="mailto:state.climatologist@umontana.edu">state.climatologist@umontana.edu</a>.
      Our mailing address is:
    </p>
    <address>
      Montana Climate Office<br>
      W.A. Franke College of Forestry &amp; Conservation<br>
      University of Montana<br>
      Missoula, MT 59812
    </address>

    <h2>Using our data</h2>
    {/* src/pages/about/agreement.astro (CC BY 4.0 for Mesonet data, the
        disclaimer for the rest); the APIs link it. */}
    <p>
      Montana Mesonet data are free to use under a Creative Commons license. The{' '}
      <a href={url('about/agreement/')}>user agreement and disclaimer</a> has the license terms and
      covers the rest of our data and services.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
